<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>Tiny house kits</strong> are packages of pre-cut or pre-built components (framing, wall panels, roof parts and sometimes windows and doors) that you or a crew assemble on site. Plans are the construction drawings only: you buy materials locally and build from scratch. In most builds, a kit saves time on the shell and costs more per square foot of material, while plans plus your own sourcing cost less in cash but take longer. Neither one is a finished house. Foundation, plumbing, electrical, insulation and interior finishes are usually on you either way, and those make up most of the bill.</p>

      <div class="key-takeaways">
        <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Most tiny house kits cover only the weather-tight shell, which is often just 25&ndash;45% of the total project cost.</li>
          <li>Typical kit prices run about $8,000&ndash;$50,000 for precut and shell kits and $25,000&ndash;$80,000+ for panelized and SIP kits, before shipping.</li>
          <li>Plans cost far less up front, and buying lumber locally usually beats kit pricing on materials, but you spend more hours cutting and sourcing.</li>
          <li>Kits win on speed and predictability for the shell. Plans win on customization, permit flexibility and total cost for patient builders.</li>
          <li>A hybrid approach (plans plus a local truss or panel supplier) gets you much of the speed of a kit without the shipping bill.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

