      <h2 id="what-are-kits">What tiny house kits are</h2>
      <p>&ldquo;Kit&rdquo; is a loose word in the tiny house world. Sellers use it for everything from a pallet of numbered lumber to a factory-built box delivered on a truck. Before you compare prices, figure out which of these four categories you are looking at.</p>

      <h3 id="precut">Precut lumber kits</h3>
      <p>The framing members arrive cut to length and labeled, with a set of assembly drawings. You still stand every wall, nail every joint and sheathe every surface. These are the cheapest kits and the closest thing to building from plans, minus the measuring and cutting. Cabin and A-frame designs are common in this format because their framing is repetitive.</p>

      <h3 id="shell">Shell kits</h3>
      <p>A shell kit adds the parts needed to get to &ldquo;dried in&rdquo;: sheathing, house wrap, roofing underlayment and often roofing, exterior doors and windows. When it is assembled you have a weather-tight empty box. Inside, it is a blank slate for insulation, wiring, plumbing and finishes.</p>

      <h3 id="panelized">Panelized and SIP kits</h3>
      <p>Wall, floor and roof sections are built in a shop and shipped as large panels. Framed panels are conventional studs and sheathing assembled off site. Structural insulated panels (SIPs) are a foam core laminated between two skins of OSB, so the insulation is built in. Panels go up fast, often in a day or two with a small crew and sometimes a crane or telehandler, but they need a dead-accurate foundation or trailer deck and leave little room for changes once they are cut.</p>

      <h3 id="prefab">Prefab and modular units</h3>
      <p>At the top end, &ldquo;kit&rdquo; sometimes means a mostly or fully finished unit built in a factory. Modular units are usually built to a state or local residential code and set on a foundation; others are built as park model RVs. These are really a purchase, not a build, and they sit closer to buying a finished tiny home than to DIY. We include them here because many searches for tiny house kits are really looking for this.</p>

