      <h2 id="included">What a tiny house kit includes and leaves out</h2>
      <p>The biggest mistake buyers make is comparing a kit price to a finished-house price. Read the inclusions list line by line. Here is what is commonly in and commonly out:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Precut kit</th><th>Shell / panel kit</th><th>Prefab unit</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Construction drawings</td><td>Assembly only; stamped drawings may cost extra</td><td>Usually; engineering often extra</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Floor system</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>Often</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wall and roof framing</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sheathing and house wrap</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roofing</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Often underlayment only</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Windows and exterior doors</td><td>Rarely</td><td>Sometimes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Insulation</td><td>No</td><td>SIPs only</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Plumbing, electrical, HVAC</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Usually</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Interior finishes, cabinets, fixtures</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Usually</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Foundation or trailer</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No (site work extra)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Freight and unloading</td><td>Extra</td><td>Extra</td><td>Extra</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Also budget for things no kit includes: permits, a site survey, utility hookups or <a href="/tools/off-grid-solar-calculator/">off-grid solar</a>, a well and septic on rural land, and tool rental. Our <a href="/blog/how-much-does-a-tiny-house-cost/">tiny house cost breakdown</a> walks through every one of these line items.</p>

      <h2 id="kit-prices">Typical tiny house kit prices</h2>
      <p>Prices swing widely with size, materials and what is included, so treat these as typical estimates for a roughly 200&ndash;400 sq ft home, not quotes:</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Kit type</th><th class="num">Typical kit price</th><th class="num">Typical total once finished</th><th>Assembly time (shell)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Precut lumber kit</td><td class="num">$8k &ndash; $30k</td><td class="num">$45k &ndash; $110k</td><td>2&ndash;6 weeks DIY</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Shell kit (dried-in)</td><td class="num">$15k &ndash; $50k</td><td class="num">$55k &ndash; $120k</td><td>1&ndash;4 weeks DIY</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Panelized / SIP kit</td><td class="num">$25k &ndash; $80k</td><td class="num">$65k &ndash; $140k</td><td>2&ndash;7 days with a crew</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Prefab / modular unit</td><td class="num">$60k &ndash; $150k+</td><td class="num">$75k &ndash; $180k+</td><td>Set in 1&ndash;2 days</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The &ldquo;total once finished&rdquo; column assumes owner-built finishing at roughly $150&ndash;$330 per square foot all-in, the same range we use across Tiny Homes Blueprint. Hiring a builder for the finish work pushes totals toward $250&ndash;$500 per square foot. Freight adds anywhere from a few hundred dollars for a regional delivery to several thousand for a long-haul flatbed, plus equipment to unload it.</p>

      <div class="callout wood">
        <strong>The math most kit buyers miss</strong>
        <p>On a 300 sq ft home that costs $60,000&ndash;$100,000 to finish, a $25,000 shell kit covers the part of the job that is fastest to build. Plumbing, electrical, insulation, cabinets and finishes take most of the hours and most of the money, kit or no kit.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="plans">Building from plans and sourcing your own materials</h2>
      <p>With plans, you get the drawings (floor plans, elevations, sections, framing and details) and you buy everything else from a lumberyard, big-box store or local suppliers. A good plan set costs a small fraction of a kit. See <a href="/blog/free-tiny-house-plans/">free vs paid tiny house plans</a> for what a complete set should include.</p>
      <h3 id="plans-advantages">Where plans come out ahead</h3>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>Lower material cost.</strong> Local lumber, bought in standard lengths and on sale when you can, usually undercuts kit pricing, which carries the cost of factory cutting, packaging and margin.</li>
        <li><strong>No freight bill.</strong> Materials come from down the road, often with free or cheap local delivery.</li>
        <li><strong>Easy changes.</strong> Want a bigger window, a different roof pitch or a porch? Change the drawing and order different parts. Changing a kit after it is cut is costly or impossible.</li>
        <li><strong>Permit-friendly.</strong> Your building department reviews drawings, not boxes of parts. A detailed plan set is what you submit either way, and it can be adjusted to local snow, wind and energy code requirements.</li>
        <li><strong>Pay as you go.</strong> You buy materials phase by phase instead of paying for the whole shell up front.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="plans-drawbacks">Where plans cost you</h3>
      <p>You do the takeoff (the material list), the cutting and the sourcing, which adds days or weeks. Material waste is higher if you are new to framing. Price swings in lumber hit you directly. And you need more tools and more confidence with a saw. If you have never built anything, read <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-tiny-house/">how to build a tiny house</a> first to see what the full process involves.</p>

      <h2 id="comparison">Tiny house kits vs plans: side-by-side</h2>
      <p>Here is how the two paths compare on the factors that usually decide it. Ratings assume an owner-builder doing most of the work on a 200&ndash;400 sq ft home.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Tiny house kit</th><th>Plans + your own materials</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Up-front cost</strong></td><td>High: you pay for the shell at once</td><td>Low: plans first, materials as you go</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Total cost</strong></td><td>Usually higher for the same house</td><td>Usually lower, if you manage waste</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Shell build time</strong></td><td>Days to a few weeks</td><td>Several weeks to a few months</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Customization</strong></td><td>Limited to the seller&rsquo;s options</td><td>High: any change before you build</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Permits</strong></td><td>May need extra engineering for your area</td><td>Drawings can be adapted to local code</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Skill needed</strong></td><td>Moderate: assembly, square and level</td><td>Higher: layout, cutting and framing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Shipping</strong></td><td>Freight plus unloading equipment</td><td>Local delivery, often free</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Waste</strong></td><td>Low: parts are cut to fit</td><td>Moderate, depends on your planning</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Risk</strong></td><td>Seller delays, missing or damaged parts</td><td>Mistakes in cutting and ordering</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Want to run your own numbers? Put your size and finish level into the <a href="/tools/tiny-house-cost-calculator/">tiny house cost calculator</a>, then compare a kit quote against the shell portion of that estimate.</p>

      <h2 id="who">Who should pick a kit, and who should pick plans</h2>
      <h3 id="pick-kit">A kit makes sense if you&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Have limited time on site, such as a remote property you can only visit for a few weeks.</li>
        <li>Want the shell up and dried in fast before winter or the rainy season.</li>
        <li>Like a seller&rsquo;s design as-is and do not need changes.</li>
        <li>Have the cash to pay for the shell in one go and can receive a large freight delivery.</li>
        <li>Are building somewhere with skilled labor in short supply, where fewer hours on site really matters.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 id="pick-plans">Plans make more sense if you&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Want to keep total cost as low as possible and are willing to trade time for money.</li>
        <li>Need to adapt a design to your lot, your climate or your family, for example flipping a layout or adding a bedroom.</li>
        <li>Are building a <a href="/tiny-house-on-wheels-plans/">tiny house on wheels</a>, where every pound and inch matters and the design has to fit your exact trailer.</li>
        <li>Plan to finance in phases and buy materials as cash comes in.</li>
        <li>Need drawings your building department will accept, especially for a home on a permanent foundation or an <a href="/adu-plans/">ADU</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="hybrid">The hybrid approach: plans plus local components</h2>
      <p>You do not have to choose one extreme. Many owner-builders buy a plan set and then order the slow, fiddly parts from local suppliers:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Engineered roof trusses</strong> from a local truss plant, built to the plan&rsquo;s spans and loads. This removes the hardest framing task.</li>
        <li><strong>Wall panels or SIPs</strong> from a regional panel shop, made from your drawings. You get panelized speed with much lower freight.</li>
        <li><strong>A precut package</strong> from a lumberyard that offers cutting services from a material list.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows and doors</strong> ordered early, since lead times often drive the schedule.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The plan set from Tiny Homes Blueprint is the common thread: the same drawings go to your permit office, your truss supplier and your framers. For example, <a href="/plans/plan-12-2417/">Plan 12-2417</a> is a single-level, 700 sq ft cabin with simple 4:12 truss framing that suits this kind of build, and <a href="/plans/plan-12-2418/">Plan 12-2418</a> adds a third bedroom and second bath on the same 20 x 38 ft footprint. Browse all <a href="/tiny-house-plans/">tiny house plans</a> or look at <a href="/small-cabin-plans/">small cabin plans</a> and <a href="/a-frame-house-plans/">A-frame plans</a>, two styles that suit precut and truss-based builds well.</p>

      <h2 id="questions">Questions to ask a tiny house kit seller</h2>
      <p>Before you send a deposit, get written answers to these:</p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><strong>What exactly is included?</strong> Ask for an itemized parts list, not a marketing summary.</li>
        <li><strong>Are the drawings stamped or engineered for my location?</strong> Snow, wind and seismic loads vary, and your building department may require an engineer&rsquo;s stamp.</li>
        <li><strong>What code is it designed to?</strong> A home on a foundation usually needs to meet the residential code your area has adopted; a home on wheels may be treated as an RV or a park model. See our <a href="/blog/tiny-house-zoning-laws/">tiny house zoning laws guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>What are the delivery cost, method and lead time?</strong> Will it come on a flatbed that needs a forklift or crane to unload?</li>
        <li><strong>What happens if parts are missing or damaged?</strong> Who pays to replace and ship them, and how fast?</li>
        <li><strong>What foundation or trailer tolerances does it need?</strong> Panels in particular need a square, level base.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I make changes, and what do they cost?</strong> Window moves, extra doors, a different roof?</li>
        <li><strong>What is the payment schedule and refund policy?</strong> Avoid paying the full price long before production.</li>
        <li><strong>Can I talk to someone who has assembled one?</strong> A seller who has delivered kits before should be able to arrange it.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Plan the layout before you choose a kit or plans</strong>
        <p>Whichever way you go, sketch how you will actually live in the space first. The free <a href="/tools/tiny-house-layout-planner/">tiny house layout planner</a> lets you test furniture and room sizes, and the <a href="/tools/how-big-should-my-tiny-house-be/">size calculator</a> helps you pick a square footage.</p>
      </div>

